- op: discuss  # e74b
  at: '2026-09-15T06:27:40Z'  # e74b
  by: agent  # e74b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e74b
  clock: 3  # e74b
  nonce: e74b  # e74b
  message: "SATISFIED in PR #969 (9a23f12923). Positive behavioural evidence, and the item's\nown filed repro re-run at tip rather than a proxy.\n\nTHE REPRO, REPRODUCED FIRST. A java fixture whose class extends a type outside\nthe analysis, so an inherited bare call does not resolve, emits exactly what the\nitem recorded 2026-08-25:\n\n    dst = java:external:0-0:doFinal:external_symbol   is_resolved=False\n    call_construct = None        <- no refusal signal\n    enclosing_class = 'App'      <- the item's option (a), confirmed present\n    receiver_type_hint = None\n\nFed to the SHIPPED registrar with the SHIPPED catalogue it produced\n{'plaintext': ['javax.crypto.Cipher.doFinal']} on App.bareCallArm. After the\nfix, the same edge is refused while the three typed-receiver calls in the same\nfixture still register. The barrier arm is alive, the phantom is gone.\n\nWHAT I SHIPPED IS NEITHER (a) NOR (b). Both proposals add a refusal signal for\none population, and the root_cause is right that the guard had none for it --\nbut the shape of the bug is that the guard enumerated ABSENCE and permitted\nwhatever it had not thought of. An absence cannot be enumerated; the PRESENCE\nset can. The fall-through is now a refusal and the permit branches are the\nwhole rule, so no per-language vocabulary (b) and no new consumer key (a) is\nneeded. enclosing_class is deliberately NOT read, and a test pins that its\npresence does not rescue the edge and its absence does not re-permit, so it is\nnever mistaken for load-bearing.\n\nTWO THINGS THE ITEM GOT WRONG, both in my favour and one in yours.\n\n1. \"exactly one refusal signal\" -- there were two, but the second is VACUOUS.\nambiguous_names is sourced from the io_primitives I/O-collision lists, and NONE\nof the nine sanitizer short names (encrypt, doFinal, Seal, deriveKey,\nderiveBits, seal_in_place, expand) appears in its own language's list, in any\nlanguage. The \"meta-absent safety net\" was empty for exactly the population it\nwas described as netting. The parameter is removed rather than left dead.\n\n2. NOT A JAVA POPULATION. python, typescript, go and rust all reached the same\nfall-through. java's lack of free functions is a SUFFICIENT reason to refuse a\nbare call, not a necessary one: every one of the twelve shipped sanitizers is\nreceiver-shaped, so binding one from a bare name with no receiver was never\njustified in any language.\n\nTHE THIRD FORM OF EVIDENCE, which I nearly shipped a regression over. \"Exact\nqualified match OR module slot\" is NOT the permit set. A call site can name its\nreceiver in the callee NAME while the module slot still holds the external\nplaceholder: Fernet.encrypt against a catalogued\ncryptography.fernet.Fernet.encrypt. My first implementation refused that and\nDELETED REAL BARRIERS; it was caught by\ntest_cli_verify_claims.py::test_verify_claims_taint_flow_confirmed, not by\nreasoning. The permit set is now three branches, the third matching on a\nseparator boundary so t.encrypt cannot bind ...Fernet.encrypt, with the\nseparator vocabulary imported from member_names rather than re-spelled.\n\nONE EARLIER RULING REVERSED, flagged because it is a reversal and not an\nextension. test_unresolved_free_function_sanitizer_registers asserted that a\nbare `encrypt` stamped call_construct=\"function\" DOES bind Fernet.encrypt, on\nthe grounds that \"the reported bug is untyped method / ambiguous collisions,\nnot free-function barriers\". That describes the scope of INV-finoh's bug\nreport, not a case that free-function binding is sound -- a free function named\nencrypt is not the method Fernet.encrypt, and the construct stamp says which\nSYNTAX was used, not which callee was resolved. This item's statement says \"for\nevery call shape\", so I took it as governing and reversed the test in place with\nthe argument written into it. If you disagree, that test is the single place to\nre-litigate it.\n\nSCOPE, COUNTED BEFORE BUILDING as the item required. Across eleven repositories\n(hypergumbo, alertmanager, killbill and eight javascript ones) the shipped\ncatalogue registers ZERO sanitizer barriers of any kind. The probe was\ncontrolled against the java fixture, where it finds four including the phantom,\nso the zeros are real and not an instrument failure. The defect was LATENT: it\narms itself with every sanitizer added, and the shipped java entry removes\n`plaintext`, which is minted only by a source whose catalogue slotting\n(module=javax.crypto, name=Cipher.doFinal) does not match what the analyzer\nemits (module=javax.crypto.Cipher, name=doFinal) -- so no java plaintext flow\nexists to suppress today. That last mismatch is a separate recall defect I did\nnot chase.\n\nDirection is safe: refusing a barrier un-suppresses, so this can only ADD\nfindings. Six fixtures that reached the barrier machinery THROUGH the fail-open\nnow carry real receiver evidence."  # e74b
- op: discuss  # 9cfe
  at: '2026-09-15T07:01:33Z'  # 9cfe
  by: agent  # 9cfe
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9cfe
  clock: 4  # 9cfe
  nonce: 9cfe  # 9cfe
  message: "OWNER RATIFIED THE REVERSED RULING. Raised with the owner that PR #969 did not\nmerely extend the prior ruling on unresolved sanitizer binding -- it overturned\none. test_taint.py::test_unresolved_free_function_sanitizer_registers asserted\nthat a bare, unresolved call to a plain function named \"encrypt\", stamped\ncall_construct=\"function\", binds the catalogued cryptography.fernet.Fernet.encrypt\nas a barrier. INV-fuduz reverses it: a bare \"encrypt\" is not \"Fernet.encrypt\",\nthe construct stamp names the SYNTAX written and not the callee RESOLVED, and the\noriginal justification described the SCOPE OF THE INV-finoh BUG REPORT rather\nthan arguing free-function binding is sound.\n\nThe owner's response: \"sounds like you made the right call.\" Settled -- do not\nre-litigate without new evidence.\n\nTHE ARGUABLE PART, disclosed and still true: INV-fuduz was filed about java,\nwhich has no free functions, and the fix generalizes to python, which does. The\ndefense is catalogue-shaped, not semantic -- all twelve shipped sanitizers are\nreceiver-shaped in every language, so no legitimate match is lost today, and a\ngenuinely bare-named sanitizer still binds through the exact-qualified-name\nbranch (pinned by test_sanitizer_receiver_evidence_required.py::\ntest_a_sanitizer_named_without_a_receiver_still_binds_bare). RE-OPEN TRIGGER: the\ncatalogue gains a real free-function sanitizer.\n\nSingle place to re-litigate: packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_taint.py:757,\nwhere the full argument is written into the docstring."  # 9cfe
